Mechanism: When bgpd/zebra stop, router1 retains only its directly connected networks. As a result, traffic from pc1 (195.11.14.2) toward pc2’s subnet (200.1.1.0/24) hits router1 (195.11.14.1), which has no route and returns ICMP Destination Net Unreachable—exactly what we observe when pinging pc2 from pc1. pc2 can still reach its default gateway (router2: 200.1.1.1) because that is local L2/L3. Router2’s kernel still shows a leftover “proto bgp” route to 195.11.14.0/24 via 193.10.11.1, creating asymmetric reachability that cannot complete 
